1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,750
Moving money off a rollup takes three transactions, not one. You start it, you prove it, and a week later you come back and finish it.

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,234
People do the first two and never come back.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:19,934
So I counted. On Base, across ten sampled days, two hundred and seventy one withdrawals were proven.

4
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:26,530
Fifteen were never finished. Sixty eight thousand dollars of tether, sitting in the bridge.

5
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:32,098
Ten of them have been there seventy five days. None is still inside a challenge period.

6
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:37,866
Here is the part that makes this fixable. Anyone can finish someone else's withdrawal.

7
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:46,618
The portal takes the prover's proof from a different caller, and the recipient is already fixed inside the withdrawal that was proven.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,222
So a stranger can push it through and cannot take it. All fifteen simulate clean from an address with no connection to any of them.

9
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,758
This is that, as a KeeperHub workflow.

10
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:09,294
It reads whether the withdrawal is finished, whether anyone proved it, who that was, which dispute game the proof rests on, and whether that game resolved.

11
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,306
Two gates. Then one write.

12
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:17,034
Here it is releasing a withdrawal that had been abandoned for three days.

13
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:25,142
The owner's balance goes from zero point zero one four ether to zero point nine six four. We never held their key.

14
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:32,018
Gas was sponsored by the keeper's KeeperHub organisation, so getting the money back cost the owner nothing.

15
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:38,174
It also refuses. Give it a withdrawal nobody proved and the first gate closes.

16
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:45,382
Give it one whose dispute game is still running and the second gate closes. No transaction either time.

17
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:56,350
The first version had only one gate. It reached for the portal every thirty minutes and was reverted. Nothing was ever sent, and it still was not something you would leave running.

18
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:02,330
So it asks the game first. This one is watching five point four ether, and waiting.

19
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:10,082
Unfinished: testnet for the demonstration, one withdrawal per workflow, and it does not find them by itself.

20
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:18,914
The survey that produced those numbers, both workflows, and a checker that reads every claim back off the chain are in the repository.
